// Practice quiz: Writing methods: parameters and return values
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so comparisons are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/writing-methods

::o1 matching call::A method is declared static double average(int a, int b). Which statement compiles and keeps the result? {
=double r \= average(4, 6);#Correct. It passes two int arguments in order and stores the double the method returns.
~double r \= average(4);#Not quite. The call must pass one argument per parameter, and average has two.
~int r \= average(4, 6);#Not quite. The method returns a double, and javac will not narrow a double into an int without a cast.
~double r \= average("4", "6");#Not quite. The parameters are int, so String arguments do not match their types.
}

::o1 return type::In a method header, what does the return type tell you? {
=The type of the value the method hands back to its caller#Correct. The return type comes before the name and names what return sends back.
~The type every parameter must have#Not quite. Each parameter declares its own type inside the parentheses.
~The type of what the method prints#Not quite. Printing and returning are different; a method can print without returning anything.
~How many times the method may be called#Not quite. A header says nothing about how often a method is called.
}

::o2 every path::A method static String sign(int n) returns "positive" when n is greater than 0 and "negative" when n is less than 0, and has nothing after those two if statements. What does javac do? {
=It reports missing return statement, because when n is 0 no return runs#Correct. Every path that can finish normally must reach a return, and the n is 0 path falls off the end.
~It compiles, and sign(0) returns null#Not quite. javac checks every path that can finish normally before the program runs, and rejects this one.
~It compiles, and sign(0) returns an empty String#Not quite. Java never invents a return value for a path that has none.
~It compiles, but sign(0) throws an exception when run#Not quite. The problem is found at compile time, so the program never runs.
}

::o2 nothing returned::A method only prints a greeting and hands nothing back. Which return type should it declare? {
=void#Correct. void means the method returns no value, so it needs no return with a value.
~String, because it prints text#Not quite. A String return type would require every path that can finish normally to return a String; only a path that throws or never finishes needs no return.
~int, the default return type#Not quite. Java has no default return type; every method declares one.
~null#Not quite. null is a value, not a type, so it cannot be a return type.
}

::o3 what becomes the parameter::Four lines each print an amount times 0.2, and only the amount differs from line to line. When you extract one method, what should become its parameter? {
=The amount, because it is the part that changes#Correct. What varies between the repeated lines becomes the parameter; the shared steps stay in the body.
~The rate 0.2, because it is a number#Not quite. The rate is the same on every line, so it can stay inside the method body.
~Nothing; the method can read the amounts from main#Not quite. A method cannot see main's local variables, so the amount must be passed in.
~The println call#Not quite. A parameter holds a value, not a statement.
}

::o3 same behavior::You extracted three repeated lines into one method and replaced them with three calls. How do you check the refactor is correct? {
=Run the program and confirm it prints exactly what it printed before#Correct. Extracting a method must not change behavior, so the output should be identical.
~Check that the program got shorter#Not quite. Shorter code can still compute something different.
~Check that the new method has a long, detailed name#Not quite. A clear name helps readers, but it does not prove the behavior stayed the same.
~Check that it compiles#Not quite. A program can compile and still print different results.
}
